 ## Overview
-This method computes the discrete opacity $\alpha_i$ for a ray segment $[t_i, t_{i+1}]$ in volume rendering by integrating a density function derived from a Signed Distance Function (SDF) $f$ and the Cumulative Distribution Function (CDF) of the logistic distribution [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of correctly modeling surface opacity by ensuring the weight function peaks at the surface intersection, thereby avoiding the bias inherent in naive SDF-to-density mappings [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard transfer functions that map scalar values directly to opacity, this approach derives opacity from the geometric gradient of the implicit surface, distinguishing it from sibling methods like [Opacity Correction for Slice Count Invariance](1277-opacity-correction-for-slice-count-invariance.md) which handle slice-count artifacts, or [Alpha-to-Weight Computation (Forward)](2373-alpha-to-weight-computation-forward.md) which maps pre-computed alpha to weights [S1, unofficial].
+This method computes the discrete opacity $\alpha_i$ for a ray segment $[t_i, t_{i+1}]$ in volume rendering by integrating a density function derived from a Signed Distance Function (SDF) $f$ and the Cumulative Distribution Function (CDF) of the logistic distribution [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of correctly modeling surface opacity by ensuring the weight function peaks at the surface intersection, thereby avoiding the bias inherent in naive SDF-to-density mappings [S1, unofficial]. It differs from sibling methods like [Opacity Correction for Slice Count Invariance](1277-opacity-correction-for-slice-count-invariance.md) by deriving opacity directly from SDF gradients rather than handling slice-count artifacts [S1, unofficial].
 
